<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-06">
    <title>Week 6</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-energy-intro">
        <title>Chapters 5 and 6</title>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 8 Covers 5.1 through 5.3.1</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 9 Covers 5.6, 5,7, and 6.1 through 6.3</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
            Note: we are taking a winding path through the textbook because the 
            discussion of energy in chapter 5, though not incorrect, can easily lead
            to some misconceptions that can come back to bite you when you're doing
            the problems. I recommend taking this path, but if you want to read
            the text straight through, note that there is a big sleight-of-hand
            happening when you go from section 5.3.2 to section 5.3.3. In particular,
            the left hand side of (5.15) is <em>not</em> the total work done on the
            system.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                       Sections 5.1 through 5.2 introduce a trick to obtain the solution
                       to the differential equation obtained from Newton's laws for 1D motion
                       when the force depends only on the position <m>x</m>. Section 5.3.2
                       then formalizes this by defining the concepts of kinetic energy and
                       work done on a particle.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In class, we will talk about how to describe the energy of a two-particle system,
                        and the concept of <term>potential energy</term> will show up. I don't think the
                        book does a great job of emphasizing that potential energy is a property of a system
                        of interacting objects and doesn't belong just to one particle. This is likely a new
                        idea for you because in most problems, the source of gravitational potential energy
                        is an interaction with the Earth, and the Earth doesn't really care about the motion
                        of a small object on its surface. You should wait to read 5.5 and beyond until
                        we have discussed this in class.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 5.6 and 5.7 introduce a graphical method to understand the motion of a particle
                        qualitatively from the just the shape of <m>U(x)</m>, no integration required. Energy
                        diagrams are ubiquitous, so look at this section carefully!
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 6.1 and 6.3 use energy diagrams to talk about small displacements of an object
                        about an equilibrium point. Small displacements about an equilibrium point are very common,
                        and the fact that we can describe them accurately with the math of simple harmonic motion
                        makes the mass on a spring an incredibly useful model. You'll see the mass on a spring 
                        again in quantum mechanics, solid-state physics, quantum field theory, and beyond.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>